' ============================================================================
'  the-long-hall.cut -- a wide forest, a slow pan, and something in the trees.
'
'  The shape of the beat:
'      pan slowly left -> right across a three-screen-wide forest
'      four layers at four DEPTHS, so the woods move against themselves
'      hold                       (the pause is what makes the reveal land)
'      reveal what is waiting on the right
'      shake                      (its weight)
'      flash white                (the moment it notices you)
'      fade to black              (out)
'
'  This is the reference scene for PARALLAX.
'
'  Watch it:  cutplay.run assets/cutscenes/default/the-long-hall.cut
'  Faster:    cutplay.run assets/cutscenes/default/the-long-hall.cut auto
' ============================================================================

scene the-long-hall
dev                      ' a showcase, not a story beat: kept out of the Storybook
storybook "Into the Woods" "A slow walk east through four depths of forest, and what is standing at the end of it."

var display$ = "alagard.ttf"
var body$    = "dungeon-mode.ttf"

' --------------------------------------------------------------- THE STAGE
' THREE SCREENS WIDE, one tall. The stage is the world; the camera only ever
' shows a screen-shaped window onto it, so all that width is somewhere to go.
stage 3168x816

' ------------------------------------------------------------ FOUR DEPTHS
' PARALLAX is the whole trick. The value is how much a layer moves WITH the
' stage as the camera travels:
'
'     1.0  pinned to the world  -- sweeps past fastest   (nearest)
'     0.0  pinned to the camera -- never appears to move (infinitely far)
'
' So distance runs DOWNWARD through these numbers, and because each layer is
' shown in turn, each one also stacks in front of the last.
'
' `fill` sizes every layer against the stage rather than a number worked out
' from the art, so the layers cannot drift out of register if the art is
' regenerated at a different size.
show sky  "bg/cut-forest-sky.png"  fill parallax 0.10   ' barely moves: the far distance
show far  "bg/cut-forest-far.png"  fill parallax 0.32   ' small trees on the horizon
show mid  "bg/cut-forest-mid.png"  fill parallax 0.62   ' the wood proper
show near "bg/cut-forest-near.png" fill parallax 1.00   ' big trunks, tearing past

' --------------------------------------------------------- START AT THE LEFT
' zoom 1 on a stage this shape shows exactly one screen, and x = 0 is the far
' left edge (the camera clamps itself inside the stage, so it can never show
' the void past the end of the art).
cam 0.0,0.5 zoom 1

fade to black over 0
music "everdark" fadein 4.0
fade from black over 2.0

font  title display$ 40
color title #cfd8c0
title "INTO THE WOODS" "east, and east, and east" for 2.5

font  body$ 20
color #e8e2d0

' ------------------------------------------------------------- THE SLOW PAN
' `async` is the point: the pan is launched and returns immediately, so the
' dialogue types WHILE the camera crosses the forest. Without it this would be
' a pan, then a pause, then some text.
'
' 24 seconds for three screens is deliberately slow -- the length IS the
' effect, and a slow pan is what gives the four depths time to separate. `ease
' inout` starts and ends gently, so it reads as walking rather than as a
' machine sliding to a stop.
async pan to 1.0,0.5 over 24.0 ease inout

say "The trunks go past close enough to touch. The hills behind barely move at all."
say "That is how you know how far the far ones are."
say "Somewhere ahead, the birds have stopped."

' Join the pan: everything after this happens with the camera at rest, at the
' eastern end of the wood.
waitall

' ------------------------------------------------------------- THE PAUSE
' The reveal needs the wood to go quiet first. A beat of nothing is what makes
' the next moment land -- take this `wait` out and the thing merely appears.
wait 1.6

' ---------------------------------------------------------------- THE REVEAL
' Placed near the right of the STAGE, which is where the camera is now looking,
' and at parallax 1 so it sits in the same plane as the near trunks. It fades
' up rather than cutting in, so it resolves out of the trees instead of being
' switched on.
show wyrm "bg/cut-wolf.png" scale 3.0 at 0.90,0.62 parallax 1.0 fade 2.4
sfx "monster-appear"

say "It has been keeping pace with you the whole way."

' ------------------------------------------------------------------ IMPACT
' The order matters. The shake is its weight coming forward; the flash is the
' moment it looks at you; the fade is you not being there any more.
shake 13 for 1.1
wait 0.5

flash white 0.6
wait 0.4

musicstop fade 2.0
fade to black over 2.5
